\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The Shapes3D task.} (a) The six factors (floor hue, wall hue, object hue, scale, shape, orientation), each row varying one factor of real dataset images with the other five held at the request's values. (b) A request is a tuple of factor values; the answer is an image rendered by the one frozen decoder, and the exact judge is the hash-pinned external classifier, which must read back all six factors. The image shown is the frozen decoder on the encoder's own feature of that row: it shows the answer format, not any arm's output. (c) The two-way hold-out on object hue $\times $ shape: grey cells have rows in the scored fit pool; the outlined cells are pair corners, whose values are each seen but never together; the hatched value (the held-out object hue and the cube) is absent from the fit pool altogether. (d) A six-factor corner is absent from the fit pool although every two-factor projection of it is present (top, left); only the joint slice (bottom) shows the hole. No score of any arm appears in this figure.}}{4}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:task-shapes}{{1}{4}{\textbf {The Shapes3D task.} (a) The six factors (floor hue, wall hue, object hue, scale, shape, orientation), each row varying one factor of real dataset images with the other five held at the request's values. (b) A request is a tuple of factor values; the answer is an image rendered by the one frozen decoder, and the exact judge is the hash-pinned external classifier, which must read back all six factors. The image shown is the frozen decoder on the encoder's own feature of that row: it shows the answer format, not any arm's output. (c) The two-way hold-out on object hue $\times $ shape: grey cells have rows in the scored fit pool; the outlined cells are pair corners, whose values are each seen but never together; the hatched value (the held-out object hue and the cube) is absent from the fit pool altogether. (d) A six-factor corner is absent from the fit pool although every two-factor projection of it is present (top, left); only the joint slice (bottom) shows the hole. No score of any arm appears in this figure}{figure.1}{}}
