\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The CMAZE-T task.} (a) One generated maze, drawn by the engine: walls, floor tinted in one of four colours (each mark carries its colour's digit), keys, closed and opened doors, the agent with the key in its pocket (at most one), the goal, and the step budget. Rule A: a door of colour $c$ needs key $c$ and consumes it. Rule B: floor tinted $c$ is forbidden while holding key $c$. Rule A is applied before rule B. (b)--(d) One transition each: the request is five words (\texttt  {w\_agent}, \texttt  {w\_key}, \texttt  {w\_door}, \texttt  {w\_tint}, \texttt  {w\_dir}) and the answer is four facts (\texttt  {legal}, \texttt  {disp}, \texttt  {event}, \texttt  {pocket}), computed by the engine and checked against an independent stepper. (b) A legal move onto floor of another tint. (c) An illegal move under rule B: the agent does not move, so after equals before and the verdict is carried by the mark. (d) The same pocket and tint as (c) with a closed door of the pocket's colour: the door consumes the key first, so the move is legal. Panels (c) and (d) differ only in the door, which is the pocket $\times $ door $\times $ tint interaction held out on this instrument. No score of any arm appears in this figure.}}{5}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:task-cmazet}{{2}{5}{\textbf {The CMAZE-T task.} (a) One generated maze, drawn by the engine: walls, floor tinted in one of four colours (each mark carries its colour's digit), keys, closed and opened doors, the agent with the key in its pocket (at most one), the goal, and the step budget. Rule A: a door of colour $c$ needs key $c$ and consumes it. Rule B: floor tinted $c$ is forbidden while holding key $c$. Rule A is applied before rule B. (b)--(d) One transition each: the request is five words (\texttt {w\_agent}, \texttt {w\_key}, \texttt {w\_door}, \texttt {w\_tint}, \texttt {w\_dir}) and the answer is four facts (\texttt {legal}, \texttt {disp}, \texttt {event}, \texttt {pocket}), computed by the engine and checked against an independent stepper. (b) A legal move onto floor of another tint. (c) An illegal move under rule B: the agent does not move, so after equals before and the verdict is carried by the mark. (d) The same pocket and tint as (c) with a closed door of the pocket's colour: the door consumes the key first, so the move is legal. Panels (c) and (d) differ only in the door, which is the pocket $\times $ door $\times $ tint interaction held out on this instrument. No score of any arm appears in this figure}{figure.2}{}}
